---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Ornament from '../../components/Ornament.astro';
import { article } from '../../data/articles';

const a = article('lean-verifiable-agents');
---

<Base title={`${a.title} — Volodymyr Pavlyshyn`} description={a.summary}>
  <article class="page essay">
    <p class="crumb meta"><a href="/writing">Writing</a> · essay · {a.date}</p>
    <h1 class="display">{a.title}</h1>
    <p class="standfirst">{a.standfirst}</p>

    <Ornament />

    <p>
      A chatbot that hallucinates says something false once. An agent that
      hallucinates <em>into its memory</em> says it again tomorrow, and next month,
      and builds on it. One fluent summary — “Anna and Lena met in Berlin on the
      morning of day 2000” — becomes a permanent belief, although the memory already
      places Anna in Lisbon that morning. Every later answer stands on a
      contradiction that nothing in the pipeline noticed.
    </p>
    <p>
      Memory is what turns a language model into an agent, and it is also what turns
      a hallucination from an incident into a liability. The usual defences do not
      reach it. Retrieval cannot tell which of two contradictory notes is right; it
      returns both, ranked by similarity. Asking a second model “is this
      consistent?” gets you a plausible verdict, which is a hallucination about a
      hallucination. Tests sample behaviour; they do not bound it.
    </p>
    <p>
      What is missing is a checker: something small, fixed and independent of the
      model, that decides what an agent may remember and what it may claim. That is
      the job Lean 4 does well.
    </p>

    <h2>A verifier has to be smaller than what it verifies</h2>
    <p>
      The test for a checker is old. Proof assistants have used it for decades:
      the part you must trust should be small enough to read. Lean has a kernel of
      that kind. Tactics, automation, macros and — now — language models can produce
      a proof any way they like; the kernel re-checks the finished term, and it does
      not care how the term was found.
    </p>
    <p>
      That asymmetry is the whole idea. Finding a proof is hard and can be left to
      something untrustworthy. Checking one is cheap and mechanical. An LLM is a good
      generator and a poor judge, so put it on the side of the line where being
      wrong costs nothing: <strong>the LLM proposes, Lean decides.</strong>
    </p>
    <p>
      Lean is also a programming language that compiles to native code, which
      matters more than it sounds. The specification, the proof and the program that
      runs are written in one language and checked by one compiler. There is no
      model in a separate tool that drifts away from the code it was meant to
      describe.
    </p>

    <h2>Three places proof belongs in an agent</h2>

    <h3>1 · At the door of memory</h3>
    <p>
      Write the agent's ontology as types and its rules about the world as
      propositions. A person is in one place at a time; an event happens where its
      participants are; a parent is older than a child. Then make the store itself
      carry a proof that its contents satisfy the rules. In sketch form:
    </p>
    <figure class="code">
      <pre><code>{`structure Fact where
  person : Person
  place  : Place
  day    : Day

-- Nobody is in two places on the same day.
def Consistent (facts : List Fact) : Prop :=
  ∀ a ∈ facts, ∀ b ∈ facts,
    a.person = b.person → a.day = b.day → a.place = b.place

-- A store cannot be built without the proof.
structure Memory where
  facts : List Fact
  ok    : Consistent facts

-- Either a new store with a new proof, or the rule the fact broke.
def Memory.insert (m : Memory) (f : Fact) : Except Violation Memory`}</code></pre>
      <figcaption>A sketch. The full certified store, with its proofs, is in the
        <a href="https://github.com/Volland/bulletproof-knowledge">bulletproof-knowledge</a> repository.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>
      The type of <code>Memory</code> is the guarantee. There is no code path that
      produces an inconsistent store, because there is no way to construct one
      without a proof that it is consistent — and the proof is checked by the kernel,
      not by the agent. When <code>insert</code> refuses Anna-in-Berlin, it says which
      rule blocked the fact, and that name goes back to the model as feedback. A
      rejection is information, not a shrug.
    </p>

    <h3>2 · In the reasoning</h3>
    <p>
      Types are good at shape and invariants. Agents also need rules and search:
      who could have met whom, what follows if we assume this, which path connects
      two events. That is logic programming's home ground, and higher-order logic
      programming — λProlog — handles the parts first-order Prolog cannot: binders,
      hypothetical questions that leave no trace (<code>pi</code> and
      <code>=&gt;</code>), relations passed as arguments.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="https://volland.github.io/lean-elpi/">lean-elpi</a> runs λProlog
      inside Lean 4. Lean types derive into ELPI and back; Lean functions are callable
      as predicates. The point for agents is the last step: when a search finds an
      answer, it can be turned into a proof term the Lean kernel checks. The search
      engine is now in the same position as the LLM — useful, fast, and outside the
      trusted base. Only the certificate is trusted.
    </p>
    <figure class="code">
      <pre><code>{`inductive Tree (α : Type) where
  | leaf
  | node (l : Tree α) (a : α) (r : Tree α)
  deriving Elpi            -- the same type, on both sides

def twice (n : Nat) : Nat := 2 * n
elpi_extern twice          -- a Lean function as an ELPI predicate

elpi_program demo
#elpi demo lp%{ twice 21 X }%     -- X = 42`}</code></pre>
      <figcaption>lean-elpi v0.3.3: one type and one function shared between Lean and λProlog.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h3>3 · In the substrate</h3>
    <p>
      An agent's memory lives in a database, and the boundary to the database is
      where many quiet failures happen: a query with a misspelled label that returns
      nothing, a write inside what was meant to be a read, a user string spliced into
      query text. Each of these can be a compile error instead.
    </p>
    <figure class="code">
      <pre><code>{`node_table Person
node_table Company (key := name)
rel_table WorksAt (from := Person) (to := Company)

-- Checked against the schema while it compiles:
-- an unknown label or property, or a string compared
-- with a number, is an error at the Cypher token.
def employees := cypher% MATCH (p:Person)-[:WorksAt]->(c:Company)
                         WHERE p.age > 30 RETURN p.name, c.name`}</code></pre>
      <figcaption><a href="https://volland.github.io/ladybug-lean/">ladybug-lean</a>: typed Cypher for LadybugDB. In <a href="https://volland.github.io/typedb-lean/">typedb-lean</a>, read and write transactions are different types, so an insert inside a read does not compile.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>
      Further down, the store's own promises can be proved. Tiramemsu, my
      bitemporal memory on SQLite, claims that it never forgets, that a correction
      replays the layers that depended on the old fact, and that its time-travel
      views agree with the log. In
      <a href="https://github.com/Volland/lean-tiramemsu">lean-tiramemsu</a> those
      are theorems: the codec, the store state machine and the query semantics,
      joins and paths included, are proven — with no <code>sorry</code>, no user
      axioms and no <code>native_decide</code>, enforced in CI by a policy checker
      that inspects the compiled environment rather than grepping the source.
    </p>

    <h2>The loop</h2>
    <p>Put together, the agent runs one loop, and every step of it is explicit:</p>
    <ol class="loop">
      <li><strong>Propose.</strong> The LLM reads, summarises and extracts candidate facts and candidate answers.</li>
      <li><strong>Check.</strong> Lean decides whether each fact keeps memory consistent, and whether each answer follows from what is stored.</li>
      <li><strong>Commit or reject.</strong> A fact enters only with a proof; an answer leaves only with a certificate. A rejection names the rule it broke.</li>
      <li><strong>Feed back.</strong> The named rule goes back into the model's context, so the next proposal is better informed than the last.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      No proof, no entry. No proof, no answer. The model is free to be creative,
      because creativity no longer reaches the store unchecked.
    </p>

    <h2>What it does not give you</h2>
    <p>
      The promise is narrow, and it should be said as narrowly as it is. A checker
      guarantees <em>consistency with your axioms</em>, not truth about the world.
      If the axioms are wrong, the agent will be consistently wrong, with proofs.
      A fact that is false but consistent — Anna in Lisbon when she was in Porto —
      goes straight through.
    </p>
    <p>
      There is also a trusted base, and it is not empty: the Lean compiler and
      runtime, the C compiler, the database engine underneath, and every part that is
      tested rather than proven. In lean-tiramemsu, for example, the SPARQL and
      Cypher front ends are differential-tested against the Rust implementation, not
      proven. Writing that list down is part of the method. A verifiable system is
      one where you can say exactly what is verified.
    </p>
    <p>
      And not every agent needs this. A drafting assistant whose output a person
      reads before it matters can live with plausible. The case for proof grows with
      how long the agent remembers, how many decisions it builds on what it
      remembers, and how expensive it is to discover a contradiction late.
    </p>

    <h2>Where to start</h2>
    <p>
      You do not need a mathematics background. Lean is learnable as a typed
      functional language first and a proof assistant second, and most of what an
      agent engineer needs is propositions about their own domain, not about real
      analysis.
    </p>
    <ul class="start">
      <li>
        <a href={a.book.href}>Bulletproof Knowledge</a> — the book: Lean 4 and λProlog
        taught from zero, ending with a complete hallucination-resistant agent. Every
        snippet compiles.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/projects#lean">The Lean 4 libraries</a> — lean-elpi, lean-tiramemsu,
        typedb-lean and ladybug-lean, all open source.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://leanpub.com/dependenttypesdttlogicholforaiagentreadyknowledgegraphs">Dependent Types &amp; Logic for AI Agent Ready Knowledge Graphs</a>
        — the shorter, conceptual companion, if you want the ideas before the code.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p class="sign">— Volodymyr Pavlyshyn, Berlin</p>
    <p class="back"><a href="/writing">← All writing</a></p>
  </article>
</Base>

